{"builder":"## Criteria the build must meet (word for word)\n1. `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]` followed by a newline and exits 0.\n2. With --tsv, commas inside a field stay literal: `printf 'name\\tnote\\nx\\thello, world\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{\"name\":\"x\",\"note\":\"hello, world\"}]` followed by a newline.\n3. With --tsv and several data rows, every row comes out in order: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n3\\t4\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"},{\"a\":\"3\",\"b\":\"4\"}]` followed by a newline.\n4. Without the flag, CSV still works as before: `printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]` followed by a newline.\n5. Without the flag, tabs are not treated as separators: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints exactly `[{\"a\\tb\":\"1\\t2\"}]` followed by a newline, which is the current output.\n6. `npm test` exits 0, and test/ contains at least one test that runs bin/csv2json.js with --tsv on tab-separated input and checks its output.\n7. README.md documents the --tsv flag with an example invocation.\n\n## Limits (non-goals)\n- Quoted fields, escaped separators, or any RFC 4180 or full TSV quoting rules. Fields are split naively, as they are today.\n- Auto-detecting the separator, or adding a general --sep/--delimiter option.\n- Changing what happens with unknown command-line arguments, or adding --help.\n- Changing JSON output formatting or how the header row is used.\n\n## Steps\n1. Read bin/csv2json.js, src/parse.js, test/csv.test.js and README.md. parse(text, sep = ',') in src/parse.js already takes a separator; the CLI currently ignores argv and always calls parse(text).\n2. In bin/csv2json.js, check whether `--tsv` appears anywhere in `process.argv.slice(2)`. If it does, call parse(text, '\\t'); otherwise call parse(text) exactly as today. Keep input from stdin only and keep the output format (`JSON.stringify(...)` plus a trailing newline) unchanged. Do not add handling for other arguments. Leave src/parse.js unchanged unless you find a real need.\n3. In test/csv.test.js (keep the existing node --test setup and existing tests), add tests that run `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` via execFileSync with tab-separated input and assert the exact stdout, covering: the basic case `a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n` -> `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]\\n`; a field containing a comma (`name\\tnote\\nx\\thello, world\\n`); multiple data rows in order. Also add a test that without the flag, tab input gives `[{\"a\\\\tb\":\"1\\\\t2\"}]\\n` (the current behaviour).\n4. In README.md, add a short section documenting `--tsv`: say it reads tab-separated input instead of CSV, with an example such as `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv`. Keep the existing CSV usage.\n5. From the repo root, run each command from criteria 1-5 yourself and compare output byte for byte, then run `npm test` and confirm it exits 0.\n6. Open one pull request with these changes.","verifier":"Work at the PR head, from the repository root. Run every command; do not judge from the diff alone. Compare outputs byte for byte (e.g. pipe into `od -c` or compare with `diff` against the expected string) so a trailing newline and exact characters are checked.\n\n## Check 1 — expect: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]` followed by a newline and exits 0.\nRun: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv > /tmp/o1; echo \"exit=$?\"; printf '%s\\n' '[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]' | cmp - /tmp/o1 && echo SAME`\nPASS: exit=0 and SAME (stdout is exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]` plus one newline).\nFAIL: any other exit code, or any difference in stdout.\n\n## Check 2 — expect: With --tsv, commas inside a field stay literal: `printf 'name\\tnote\\nx\\thello, world\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{\"name\":\"x\",\"note\":\"hello, world\"}]` followed by a newline.\nRun: `printf 'name\\tnote\\nx\\thello, world\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv | cmp - <(printf '%s\\n' '[{\"name\":\"x\",\"note\":\"hello, world\"}]') && echo SAME`\nPASS: SAME printed.\nFAIL: cmp reports a difference, or the command errors.\n\n## Check 3 — expect: With --tsv and several data rows, every row comes out in order: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n3\\t4\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"},{\"a\":\"3\",\"b\":\"4\"}]` followed by a newline.\nRun: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n3\\t4\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv | cmp - <(printf '%s\\n' '[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"},{\"a\":\"3\",\"b\":\"4\"}]') && echo SAME`\nPASS: SAME printed.\nFAIL: any difference (missing row, wrong order, extra output) or an error.\n\n## Check 4 — expect: Without the flag, CSV still works as before: `printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]` followed by a newline.\nRun: `printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js | cmp - <(printf '%s\\n' '[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]') && echo SAME`\nPASS: SAME printed.\nFAIL: any difference or an error.\n\n## Check 5 — expect: Without the flag, tabs are not treated as separators: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints exactly `[{\"a\\tb\":\"1\\t2\"}]` followed by a newline, which is the current output.\nRun: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js | cmp - <(printf '%s\\n' '[{\"a\\tb\":\"1\\t2\"}]') && echo SAME` (here `\\t` must be the two literal characters backslash and t, i.e. the JSON escape; the single-quoted `'%s\\n'` argument keeps them literal).\nPASS: SAME printed — output is the JSON array with one key `a\\tb` (JSON tab escape) and value `1\\t2`, plus one newline.\nFAIL: any difference, e.g. the output splits into keys a and b.\n\n## Check 6 — expect: `npm test` exits 0, and test/ contains at least one test that runs bin/csv2json.js with --tsv on tab-separated input and checks its output.\nRun: `npm test; echo \"exit=$?\"`, then `grep -n -- '--tsv' test/*.js` and read each matching test.\nPASS: exit=0, and at least one test in test/ executes bin/csv2json.js with the `--tsv` argument, feeds it input containing tab characters, and asserts on its output.\nFAIL: npm test exits non-zero, or no test in test/ runs bin/csv2json.js with --tsv on tab-containing input and asserts on the output (e.g. only parse() is tested directly, or the output is not checked).\n\n## Check 7 — expect: README.md documents the --tsv flag with an example invocation.\nRun: `grep -n -- '--tsv' README.md` and read README.md.\nPASS: README.md mentions the --tsv flag, explains it is for tab-separated input, and shows an example invocation using it (such as `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv`).\nFAIL: no mention of --tsv, or no example invocation that uses it.\n\n## Advisory\n- Not part of PASS/FAIL: check the flag also works when not first among arguments (e.g. `node bin/csv2json.js foo --tsv`), and that src/parse.js behaviour for existing callers is unchanged.\n- Note any scope creep against the issue's non-goals (a general --sep option, --help, quoting rules, changed output formatting) as a remark only."}